#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "522a785c-3e1e-5515-82c9-8980b448bee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10553" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6ad02a19-7b07-5e07-8388-b46fd1cc48ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-10554" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "37af544e-feab-52b0-bcef-887c723093b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10764" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afa44c40-cd6e-55f0-bd40-6a0c511ea31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326565} {:answer/id #uuid "5253cc7d-57b3-5804-9d16-bd5e8bd6d2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326566} {:answer/id #uuid "b98ff884-2527-5c3f-8422-a8be4a32dad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326567} {:answer/id #uuid "f94b0f81-d7bf-564c-a005-3246df866d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326568} {:answer/id #uuid "1290deab-c050-514a-9ec9-0b344ccf443f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1290deab-c050-514a-9ec9-0b344ccf443f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326569} :answer-field/id #uuid "28577854-a611-5098-b135-796f07c42fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b85f51a-1479-5d27-ba6d-960b7e7827ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-152232" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/bdf8e7dad9550977ad6bd207b43cd75e91226ed84c2807dac3d023470d9e02ce.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5d96b1-a8ec-5386-a28b-5f239f32793c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326572} {:answer/id #uuid "61088c85-bd82-5d49-acc5-83290bf1e690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326573} {:answer/id #uuid "136bd168-ff05-5233-92c8-941fb9319dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326574} {:answer/id #uuid "67996f02-fe7c-52ea-8517-cd6b1dab226e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326575} {:answer/id #uuid "9c96ed79-34ef-58df-a79d-4f94df5088ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61088c85-bd82-5d49-acc5-83290bf1e690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326573} :answer-field/id #uuid "e645239e-b10a-588e-b4d8-879f665b50fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9cce3239-db43-5807-a173-d2b8c80eaf32" :question/math-academy-id "q-152260" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{5}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{5}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/3cb8903ed53a713c5e23e4dbad4801788440954ac3a5f7e08ab1bd71cc9c631f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3bd3888-4147-5cca-87d6-ef52e7d33408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317042} {:answer/id #uuid "11ba0022-8ee0-5b7b-a189-37ed3554abd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317043} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b039e2-90ed-53ae-80fa-ab83412812cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317044} {:answer/id #uuid "77087391-186d-5d91-931a-5c8ac49a7c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317045} {:answer/id #uuid "f422bd08-9f0c-5578-ab9f-83881b07eecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f422bd08-9f0c-5578-ab9f-83881b07eecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317046} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aacc6ce-36f1-5059-9e7e-0497d1596a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30e19b72-0416-5b80-938a-cf37e67f6c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-152265" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{e}^{u}]_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcd669c5-15c8-58a2-8b64-7ef90c4a3660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301313} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4915c4-b96b-5786-9e1d-7b9cba4d0491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301314} {:answer/id #uuid "ba37b007-40c8-5909-9627-1e2981b3f99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301315} {:answer/id #uuid "10c5eeb2-e0fc-5da5-95da-ed99d9cc86e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301316} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9b5cac-d412-5e11-a273-42a30a5cebd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4915c4-b96b-5786-9e1d-7b9cba4d0491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301314} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaae668d-9b89-5711-9627-f309743a340a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "969bf2aa-8cb3-5087-a3e3-258b63062534" :question/math-academy-id "q-152875" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({5}^{1}-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186