lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{4}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =5({e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e6117bf-ea63-50eb-9123-8e2c27dc9060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326593} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7be354-fc95-593c-aeb6-b44ac5239283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326594} {:answer/id #uuid "036ccdc8-39a2-5db7-8d6c-02d7b53759f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326595} {:answer/id #uuid "4902fa59-a76f-512a-a4cd-5ad55ac31b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326596} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6818f8-82f9-5c47-81ae-aa441183e5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4902fa59-a76f-512a-a4cd-5ad55ac31b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326596} :answer-field/id #uuid "02b6eb07-7d70-585b-9dfb-1a3075aff8e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa935b6d-d9c0-544a-ab7d-fb7401a96c66" :question/math-academy-id "q-20875" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{2x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/b350aaef1440524297a84168de46be1171b7686d9937ef5bbfc5cd55835ea54f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6ee2fd-f367-5a9d-9011-e376d0335062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c6ee2fd-f367-5a9d-9011-e376d0335062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326600} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceae2017-c078-5f16-95d5-49cf66dfe0bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e1c41ff-fb61-59f8-a754-c67ee9843025" :question/math-academy-id "q-346606" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd52100c-d961-5948-96c4-fedef99cf357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd52100c-d961-5948-96c4-fedef99cf357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326603} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b070c2-84e4-5b0f-abfe-1ba3c58ce403" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e02afc49-e916-5a0f-bd63-52014a86ba1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-350831" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-5a}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-5x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/5010180e9cd41608abc630cd8974cc69ee78337bcb46a7f079d58eeb8dda9cc2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d182f3-9d75-54c3-b417-f852e1ef78eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d182f3-9d75-54c3-b417-f852e1ef78eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317077} :answer-field/id #uuid "b096aed0-3798-522b-84a3-5f4f8d7e125f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8db43b84-59e1-5d8b-9409-aff2fd00efef" :question/math-academy-id "q-351416" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(8-{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/c2af905a46cea7b7512a727a5e53a2a178e4902f7114f86439365684e6685efa.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d2b1a32-5ab9-588d-80de-b21ffe231aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d2b1a32-5ab9-588d-80de-b21ffe231aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301358} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2c721b-d336-528e-8596-b9b78c5776b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7dd0fc4-1e7b-59a0-83e5-4685362a10c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351513" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37cacb50-acde-5f4c-98ec-48ef18e314f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301361} {:answer/id #uuid "81bf3d4b-df09-53f0-9589-1ccd022fa30e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301362} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce5ebf5-45af-590f-b079-aa0e1b5bd638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37cacb50-acde-5f4c-98ec-48ef18e314f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301361} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad63441b-0d19-55c1-8d5b-d23447518684" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "680b7856-af9d-5109-9fd9-00152a4dd785" :question/math-academy-id "q-351523" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{4}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{4}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/e50ca6e9326f5a6146a7a486adcc2d3d5f5c059eae3e2e1b06fbff8664b1c7df.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caf178c6-ee17-560f-9c52-0b174654b01b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326607} {:answer/id #uuid "6b63ef24-a739-56ad-b07f-5acb34cecd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b63ef24-a739-56ad-b07f-5acb34cecd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326608} :answer-field/id #uuid "719dc9c1-bc0e-5fc4-b08e-c41f599d575f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "875dd4ee-1f0c-53fd-8431-b98eef8a3afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-351528" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/34abda46dc9fc2c6f14ce4f8460ba076a16004bb9141eb8074cdc08e0c12fd2f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b08830f-a367-50a0-83be-76b3a7ff2f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326611} {:answer/id #uuid "17eeab63-b8f9-5f27-8f93-ae65c815e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17eeab63-b8f9-5f27-8f93-ae65c815e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326612} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5de2972-529c-5a34-816d-666268bf56f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e17511d0-70be-57e1-9bd3-bc3b0ce3ce74" :question/math-academy-id "q-351559" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-3x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/998ec723fd7212da4382718750465ce313349ed3f2c0258cc1f68e0a5485d968.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
